  "strategy": "1. Approaches and Rationale\n   We develop a deep learning-based approach to learn an optimal function h(x) that minimizes the maximum overlap. The key observation is that the problem can be framed through the lens of convolutional neural networks, where the objective function is the maximum correlation value. \n\n   We'll train a neural network to output a function h(x) that minimizes the overlap metric while satisfying the constraints (0\u2264h(x)\u22641 and sum(h)=1). The network structure includes convolutional layers to capture local patterns and a regularization term to enforce the sum constraint.\n\n   This non-gradient approach offers several benefits over traditional optimization:\n   - Significantly faster convergence using transfer learning\n   - Ability to generalize from lower resolutions to higher ones\n   - Less susceptibility to local minima\n   - More parallelizable computation\n\n   The method's limitations are:\n   - Requires careful architecture design\n   - The neural network learns patterns from optimal construction, which must be validated\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\n   Variables:\n   - Input: xs \u2208 \u211d^n (domain points)\n   - Output: h_pred \u2208 [0,1]^n (function values)\n   - Lag space: lags \u2208 {-n+1, ..., n-1}\n\n   Objective function:\n   C5(x) = dx * max_{lags}(\u222b h(x)(1-h(x+k))dx)\n\n   Constraints:\n   - h(x) \u2208 [0,1] for all x\n   - \u222bh(x)dx = 1\n\n   We approximate the problem with:\n   minimize \u03b8   max_k ||corr(h_theta,k) - M||_2\n   subject to:  \u03a3(h_theta)*dx = 1\n               0\u2264h_theta(x)\u22641\n\n   Where:\n   - corr(h_theta,k) is the correlation at lag k\n   - M is a vector of target maximum correlations\n   - \u03b8 is learnable parameters\n\n   We use a Feed-Forward Neural Network with:\n   - Multiple fully-connected layers\n   - Tanh activation function for output constraints\n   - Learnable temperature to control feature activation\n\n   The error function is defined as:\n   Ladder = MSE(correlation(h_theta), np.log(constraint_targets))\n\n3. Implementation Procedure\n   a. Setup Function Space:\n      project_feasible(h, n): clip values to [0,1] and adjust to satisfy sum constraint\n      correlation_targets = [max(initial_correlation) with appropriate adjustment\n\n   b. Network Architecture:\n      Input: flattened initial_parent (400 samples)\n      Architecture: \n         - Dense(256) -> Tanh\n         - Dense(128) -> Tanh\n         - Dense(64) -> Tanh\n         - Dense(n//4) -> Tanh\n         - Dense(n) -> Tanh\n      Output: h_predictions (n values)\n\n   c. Objective Function:\n      def smooth_overlap(h):\n          corr_values = np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode='full')\n          max_corr = np.max(corr_values)\n          adjusted = np.clip(corr_values - max_corr + 5.0, -5, 5)\n          return np.log(np.mean(np.exp(adjusted))) / n_lags\n\n      def compute_loss(h):\n          sum_constraint = np.abs(np.sum(h)*dx - 1)\n          return smooth_overlap(h) + 500.0*sum_constraint\n\n   d. Training Loop:\n        Use Adam optimizer with:\n          - Learning rate = 0.001\n          - Training for 1000 epochs\n          - Batch size = 32\n          - Early stopping if no improvement\n\n   e. Export Operation:\n        Save final model weights to \"erdos_model.h5\"\n\n4. Search Choices and Variations  \n   Core Parameters:\n     - Temperature schedule: 0.5 to 0.1 over 20 epochs\n     - Loss weights: sum constraint 500 times stronger than overlap\n     - Batch normalization at input layers\n\n   for resolution levels 400, 800, 1200:\n     - Use transfer learning from coarser resolution\n     - Warm-up by training initial weights for 50 epochs\n     - Use cyclic learning rate from 1e-4 to 5e-4\n     - Add random noise injection (Dropout) in training\n\n5. Compute Budget and Robustness\n   Timing:\n     - 400-point resolution: 30 seconds\n     - 800-point resolution: 75 seconds\n     - 1200-point resolution: 120 seconds\n   Total budget: 1300 seconds\n\n   Optimization Checkpoints:\n     - After 20% elapsed time: save model\n     - Speed depends on resolution level\n     - Use validation split for early stopping\n\n   Fallback Strategy:\n     - Use averaged versions of previous checkpoints\n     - Continue training until improvement stalls\n     - Maximum epochs: 1000 for each resolution\n     - Reduce learning by 50% and train further if improvement < 0.95*previous_best\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract\n   Final Checks:\n     - Project output to feasible space: 0\u2264h\u22641 and sum=1\n     - Re-score final output using: \n          dx=2/n_points\n          corr = np.correlate(h,1-h,mode='full')\n          c5 = dx * np.max(corr)\n     - Ensure all time windows were respected\n     - Document final training and resolution used\n\n   Format return: (numpy_array(h), np.max(corr)*dx, n_points)",
